WEBVTT

00:00:02.600 --> 00:00:05.100
A deal team asks a question
like this every week.

00:00:05.300 --> 00:00:08.700
Answering it properly means hours
inside 10-Ks and 10-Qs.

00:00:10.800 --> 00:00:14.300
DiligenceIQ reads the filings for you,
and shows its evidence.

00:00:16.000 --> 00:00:18.550
Open a company, and the first read
is already there:

00:00:18.550 --> 00:00:21.000
how it's performing, and what changed.

00:00:23.300 --> 00:00:29.700
Like the $4.5 billion export-control charge
that pulled NVIDIA's gross margin to 60.5%.

00:00:29.700 --> 00:00:32.200
Flagged, with the filing passage behind it.

00:00:34.400 --> 00:00:36.450
Then ask anything, in plain English.

00:00:36.450 --> 00:00:40.300
It searches the filings, gathers the
passages that answer your question,

00:00:40.300 --> 00:00:43.400
and makes exactly one model call
to write the brief.

00:00:43.600 --> 00:00:45.000
Every step is shown.

00:00:46.600 --> 00:00:48.800
What comes back reads like
an investment memo:

00:00:48.800 --> 00:00:51.300
a summary, the key findings, and the trend.

00:00:54.900 --> 00:00:57.300
Every claim opens the passage
it came from.

00:00:58.600 --> 00:01:01.800
And every number is checked
against the filing it cites.

00:01:03.000 --> 00:01:04.650
Compare companies side by side:

00:01:04.650 --> 00:01:08.000
which risk areas they share,
and where their trends diverge.

00:01:11.300 --> 00:01:14.000
A cited brief in about a minute,
for about thirteen cents.

00:01:14.100 --> 00:01:16.200
And close to nothing
when no one is using it.

00:01:17.300 --> 00:01:19.200
Today, it reads public filings.

00:01:19.200 --> 00:01:22.300
Next, it watches for new ones.
And later, your deal room.

00:01:22.500 --> 00:01:23.700
DiligenceIQ.

00:01:23.700 --> 00:01:25.650
Know what changed. Know what matters.

00:01:25.650 --> 00:01:27.600
Know what to investigate next.
